Expected observation: Batch insertion will generally be faster for large datasets because it reduces the number of database communication round trips. Statement may be slower because it constructs a new SQL command for every row. PreparedStatement reuses a parameterized SQL statement, but executing it individually still involves one execution per record.

Actual timings depend on hardware, database configuration, indexes, network latency and JDBC driver settings.
B3.3 – Auto-commit vs Transaction (2 Marks)
Objective: Demonstrate what happens when an error occurs halfway through inserting 10,000 records.
1. Complete the given PassLoader code (TODO 1–3)
The following is the completed version of the assignment's supplied code. It executes batches of 1,000 rows and commits only after all records have been inserted successfully.

import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.util.List;

public class PassLoader {

    /**
     * Loads all passes in one transaction, in batches of 1,000 rows.
     * Returns the number of rows loaded.
     */
    public static int loadAll(
            Connection con, List<String[]> passes)
            throws SQLException {

        String sql =
            "INSERT INTO bus_pass(pass_id, reg_no, name, route_no, amount) "
            + "VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";

        boolean oldAutoCommit = con.getAutoCommit();
        con.setAutoCommit(false);

        try (PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            int n = 0;

            for (String[] p : passes) {

                // TODO 1: Set parameters and add the row to the batch
                ps.setString(1, p[0]);
                ps.setString(2, p[1]);
                ps.setString(3, p[2]);
                ps.setInt(4, Integer.parseInt(p[3]));
                ps.setDouble(5, Double.parseDouble(p[4]));

                ps.addBatch();
                n++;

                if (n % 1000 == 0) {
                    ps.executeBatch();
                    ps.clearBatch();
                }
            }

            // Execute any remaining rows
            ps.executeBatch();
            ps.clearBatch();

            // TODO 2: Commit and return the number of rows
            con.commit();
            return n;

        } catch (SQLException | RuntimeException e) {

            // TODO 3: Roll back every insert in this transaction
            con.rollback();
            throw e;

        } finally {
            con.setAutoCommit(oldAutoCommit);
        }
    }
}



Explanation of the TODOs:
- TODO 1: setString(), setInt() and setDouble() assign the five values to the SQL parameters. addBatch() queues each record. executeBatch() runs the queued records after every 1,000 entries.
- TODO 2: commit() makes all inserted records permanent. The method returns the number of records processed.
- TODO 3: rollback() undoes all changes made in the transaction if an SQL or runtime error occurs. The exception is re-thrown so the calling program knows the operation failed.
The table must use the InnoDB storage engine for MySQL transaction rollback to work.
2. Generate 10,000 passes and force a failure halfway
Use the following test code:
List<String[]> passes = PassBenchmark.generatePasses(10000);

// Insert an invalid amount at the halfway position
passes.set(5000, new String[] {
    "P5001", "REG5001", "Student5001", "5", "INVALID_AMOUNT"
});

try (Connection con = PassBenchmark.connect()) {

    try (Statement st = con.createStatement()) {
        st.executeUpdate("DELETE FROM bus_pass");
    }

    try {
        PassLoader.loadAll(con, passes);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Insertion failed: " + e.getMessage());
    }

    try (Statement st = con.createStatement();
